bkc = """K, not L_n, is classical: K = N(1/a + rho(R_s1+...+R_sn+[N]) + 1/a), rho = 90-degree rotation, R_s = lift of a box of slope s (odd denominator); h = rotation fixing the middle tangle, swapping the two 1/a columns (CHECKED 12/12, Jones vs spherogram). n=1: 3-tangle Montesinos; n>=2, |a|>=3: two-node arborescent, Sigma_2 a graph manifold with one JSJ torus, so not Montesinos (PROVED); hyperbolic (strict angle structures, E1-E6). L_n = T(1/n) is in NONE of these classes: det 1, one rational-tangle replacement from the unknot (L_+-2: unknotting number one; L_+-1: one band move); Sigma_2(L_n) = S^3_{1/n}(K) is hyperbolic (rigorous for |n|>=9, numerical for |n|<=3), so L_n is not arborescent, Montesinos, pretzel or 2-bridge. Not alternating or quasi-alternating. Adequacy is mixed."""
pr = [
"Lickorish-Thistlethwaite, Comment. Math. Helv. 63 (1988) 527-539: semi-adequacy; reduced Montesinos diagrams are semi-adequate, so the adequate end of V has coefficient +-1. This covers the Montesinos K themselves (n=1, a=+-1), not the branch knots L_n, whose Sigma_2 is hyperbolic.",
"Stoimenow, J. reine angew. Math. 657 (2011) 1-55 (arXiv math/0606255): semi-adequate links have nontrivial V (Montesinos, closed 3-braids and positive links included), as do Whitehead doubles of semi-adequate knots. OVERLAP is exactly the semi-adequate L_n: 90/480 branch knots in the a2!=0 census and 4/1516 in the a2=0 census have an A- or B-adequate natural or regina-simplified diagram. Some members are certified NOT semi-adequate: E6 for all n>=1, E7 at n=1.",
"Thistlethwaite, Topology 26 (1987) and Invent. Math. 93 (1988): adequate links have nontrivial V. Futer-Kalfagianni-Purcell, LNM 2069 (2013): standard Montesinos diagrams are A- or B-adequate. Neither applies in general: 322/1516 a2=0 branch knots are certified non-adequate (an extreme coefficient of modulus >=2, or a same-sign second coefficient).",
"Dasbach-Lowrance, Comm. Anal. Geom. 26 (2018) (arXiv 1604.03501), and Lowrance-Spyropoulos, NYJM 23 (2017) (arXiv 1707.05443): Turaev genus one and almost alternating links have an extreme Jones coefficient of modulus 1, and V is nontrivial. This excludes E6's L_n, whose extreme coefficients are both of modulus 2.",
"Tuzun-Sikora, JKTR 27 (2018) (22 crossings) and arXiv 2003.06724 (24 crossings): computer verification. Only L_n with crossing number <=24 are covered. Since span V <= c, and span V(L_n) = n+28 for E6, infinitely many members lie beyond.",
"Sikora, Canad. J. Math. 76 (2024) 707-727 (arXiv 2005.08162): the Jones conjecture is equivalent to the bracket detecting rational tangles; brackets of rational tangles are q-rationals. Lemma Q is the restriction to tangles with an unknotted rational closure. No follow-up on strongly invertible knots in his arXiv listing through Oct 2026.",
"Hedden-Watson, Amer. J. Math. 132 (2010) (arXiv 0805.4423): Khovanov homology detects the unknot among tangle-unknotting-number-one knots, which include every L_n; the Jones version is stated open. Kronheimer-Mrowka (IHES 2011) with Gordon-Luecke, Watson (Adv. Math. 313, 2017) and KWZ (2022, 2026) give categorified Lemma Q for every (K,h). None of these yields a Jones statement.",
"Mullins, Topology 32 (1993), with Casson's surgery formula and the signature bound (board ODD-CLOSURE-REPAIR; each ingredient is classical, the combined statement is not found in print): a2(K)!=0 gives V(L_n)!=1 for all even n and all but at most one odd n. a2=0 holds for 9,742 of 516,600 V3 closures (1.9%), e.g. E1, E4 and E6 (a2 rechecked from Alexander polynomials).",
"Arborescent knots: no theorem that V detects the unknot among arborescent knots was found; Tuzun-Sikora use algebraic tangles only computationally, and Futer-Gueritaud (Proc. LMS 2009) is about hyperbolicity. These results cannot cover L_n anyway: arborescent knots have graph-manifold Sigma_2 (Bonahon-Siebenmann, Montesinos), while Sigma_2(L_n) is hyperbolic for |n|>=9 (Lackenby-Meyerhoff, Invent. Math. 191 (2013)).",
"Carmi-Cohen, arXiv 2606.22410 (June 2026, math.GM, unrefereed), claims the full Jones unknot conjecture via finite-type coefficients. Unverified; compare the project's FT-DEEP. If correct it subsumes everything here. Korzun-Lanina-Sleptsov, arXiv 2402.02553 (4-braids), has no detection theorem."]
det = """PARTLY_KNOWN. The statement 'V(T(1/n))!=1 for all n!=0 and all V3-class (K,h)' is not in the literature. No Jones-level quotient detection is known for any infinite hyperbolic class. Subfamilies are already covered: semi-adequate L_n (Stoimenow), a2!=0 (all even n, all but at most one odd n), crossing number <=24 (Tuzun-Sikora). The Khovanov analogue is fully known. What is new is the a2=0 core, uniformly in n. Example E6: a=-3, N=-2, box slopes 1/9, 5/13, -4/13, a2=0, K hyperbolic. The TL identity gives <L_n> = A^n P + (-A^-3)^n Q (checked against direct brackets). So for every n>=1 both extreme coefficients of V(L_n) have modulus 2: L_n is not semi-adequate and not Turaev genus one. Also span V = n+28 > 24, and Sigma_2 is hyperbolic for n>=9. No known theorem covers these knots. Caveat: each single (K,h) is a two-channel family, settled by a finite computation; the theorem's content is uniformity over infinitely many K. Method comparison: box vectors are 2x2 continued-fraction transfer matrices, as in Lee-Schiffler 2019, Morier-Genoud-Ovsienko q-rationals, Kogiso-Wakui and Sikora Thm 1.7, here in a mixed-colour Gamma_0(2) form. The jet automaton is a finite-state head/tail analysis, akin to adequacy, Dasbach-Lin and Bae-Morton extreme terms. The Farkas certificates resemble the integer-programming degree bounds of Garoufalidis-van der Veen 2016 and GLV 2020. So the ingredients are known and the combination is new."""
print(len(bkc),[len(x) for x in pr],len(det))
